## Routines and reminders

- keep a daily routine
- use calendars, notes, pill organisers and phone reminders
- label cupboards and keep important things in the same place

## Staying active and connected

Keep doing things you enjoy. Physical activity, hobbies and seeing people help wellbeing. Many areas have **memory cafes** and dementia-friendly activities.

## Safety at home

Telecare, such as sensors, alarms and GPS trackers, can help people live at home safely. See [home adaptations and equipment](/support/home-and-equipment/).

## Plan ahead

- make a **lasting power of attorney (LPA)** for money and for health, while you are able to. Once someone lacks capacity, it is too late
- make a **will**, and think about **advance care planning**
- talk to your family about your wishes

## Driving

You must tell the **DVLA** if you have dementia. Many people can keep driving for a while, depending on their assessment.

## For carers

Caring for someone with dementia is demanding. Look after yourself, ask for a carer's assessment, and use breaks (respite). See [help for carers](/support/carers/).

## Support

**Alzheimer's Society** runs Dementia Connect support, and **Dementia UK** runs a helpline with Admiral Nurses.
